  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Print Dialog Settings**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the build\'s success. Now, I\'m carefully examining the print dialog screenshot. The user\'s settings are: Landscape layout, A4 paper size, a 100% custom scale, and headers/footers/background graphics are enabled. It looks like the standard dialog, but I\'m sensing something else.\n\n\n**Confirming Parent Window Print**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the key: the \"TSG Business | Google AI Studio\" header reveals the user is printing the *AI Studio Preview Frame*, not the inner iframe directly. The \"Xu\341\272\245t PDF\" button calls `window.print()` after a toast, however, the direct print call may be due to a top-level window print, or the user pressed the command shortcut for print! Chrome\'s behaviour seems to be overriding the expected iframe-specific print output, resulting in the parent window being printed! This is critical. I\'m focusing on why, and looking into the effects of iframe embedding on print behavior!\n\n\n**Investigating iframe Print Limitations**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the limitations of iframe printing. It seems the issue is that when an iframe, specifically the AI Studio preview frame, is printed directly, only the visible section is captured. This height restriction likely explains the one-page cutoff! I\'m considering recommending that users open the application in a new tab using the \"Open in new tab\" icon. However, there\'s another point: I\'m also now considering the cross-origin aspect and wondering if the `window.print()` call *within* the iframe might be the parent window\'s dialog. This changes things.\n\n\n**Clarifying Cross-Origin Print**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial discovery: the user\'s manual Ctrl/Cmd+P keyboard shortcut is the root cause. This bypasses the intended `window.print()` call within the iframe and triggers the parent window\'s print behavior, resulting in the parent URL appearing in the footer. It is not a cross-origin printing issue. I was originally thinking the application was not cross-origin. If the user had clicked the \"Xu\341\272\245t PDF\" button, the expected iframe print would have occurred. I must instruct the user to click the \"Xu\341\272\245t PDF\" button, instead of using the keyboard shortcut.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 9
      nanos: 875428788
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
