  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing React 18 Issue**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on a `react-dom` development build issue in React 18. Specifically, when `enableSchedulingProfiler` is true or during standard dev render, React passes a `detail` object to `performance.measure` for DevTools timings. This is interesting. I will investigate further.\n\n\n**Investigating Large Data Cloning**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a \"DataCloneError\" in React 18, linked to excessive data size during performance measurements, especially when uploading large Excel files. The issue likely stems from the `data` array used for rendering tables, potentially reaching 100,000 items. Currently, I\'m exploring solutions. Can the array be avoided or lazily loaded? Short-term, disabling profiling or a production build is an option, but I want a true fix. There must be a specific component causing it.\n\n\n**Tracing Data Source Error**\n\nI\'m now tracing the origin of the problematic `data`. It comes from an Excel file processed by `XLSX.utils.sheet_to_json`, which produces plain JavaScript objects. However, even though they should be serializable, I\'m still hitting out-of-memory errors with large files, likely due to the size of the resulting `json` array. I\'ve considered memoizing tables or rows but this won\'t prevent the React DevTools from trying to clone the `data` state in `App` and cause the OOM error. A known React DevTools bug related to large state objects may be involved if this happens in a user\'s browser.\n\n\n**Examining DashboardView.tsx**\n\nI\'m now investigating `DashboardView.tsx` again, suspecting excessive re-renders might be the root cause of the crashes. I\'m carefully reviewing the component for any potential pitfalls, like accidentally storing a function or non-serializable value within its state or DOM, but the code appears standard React. Then I remembered the `recharts` issue of accidentally passing event objects to state, which triggers a `DataCloneError`. I\'m checking for similar mistakes, particularly the use of `Set` in `App.tsx` and whether the environment\'s `structuredClone` supports it or not.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Print-Mode Issues**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how print mode affects `DashboardView.tsx`. Initially, I thought a massive DOM tree due to `max-h-[400px]` was the issue, but then I realized the full table might always be rendered. My attention is now on the nested `map` within the conditional rendering of incident items in `DashboardView`. The `print:block` addition in `App.tsx` further complicates things. I will be looking closely at the conditional rendering of incident items. I\'m checking the specific implementation of the table\'s `map` function within the print context.\n\n\n**Considering Recharts Impact**\n\nI\'m now revisiting `recharts`, but with a new perspective, considering its `ResponsiveContainer` and `ResizeObserver`. I realized these might interact badly with React DevTools and print mode. Since the `ResponsiveContainer` uses a `ResizeObserver`, which detects the layout changes introduced in print mode, the chart updates its internal state to reflect the new dimensions, and React DevTools attempts to measure, potentially causing problems with large state objects. If I could hide the charts in print mode, I\'d solve the problem, but the user *needs* the charts.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 12
      nanos: 660735073
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
